.TH "mbedded\-cbor\-path" "7" "2026-11-08" "mbedded\-cbor 0.9.0" "mbedded\-cbor"
.SH Paths
A path reads one value from CBOR bytes, with no decode of the rest.
The syntax is JSONPath (RFC 9535).
basics.md states how errors come back and how long a result stays valid.
A message of \f[CR]cbor::schema<T>\f[R] has an \f[CR]at_path\f[R] of its
own (schema.md).
The reference with examples that run in the browser:
https://asmod4n.github.io/mbedded\-cbor/path.html.
.SS 1. Read one value
.IP
.EX
\f[I]// bytes: {\(dquser\(dq: {\(dqid\(dq: 42, \(dqscore\(dq: 2.5, \(dqadmin\(dq: true, \(dqname\(dq: \(dqann\(dq}}\f[R]
\f[B]auto\f[R] const id = cbor::at_path<\(dq$.user.id\(dq, std::int64_t>(bytes);
\f[B]if\f[R] (id)
    use(*id);                                                \f[I]// 42\f[R]
cbor::at_path<\(dq$.user.score\(dq, double>(bytes);                \f[I]// 2.5\f[R]
cbor::at_path<\(dq$.user.admin\(dq, bool>(bytes);                  \f[I]// true\f[R]
cbor::at_path<\(dq$.user.age\(dq, std::int64_t>(bytes).error();    \f[I]// key_not_found\f[R]
.EE
.PP
The path is a string literal, and the compiler checks it: a path that is
not valid does not compile.
\f[CR]bytes\f[R] is a \f[CR]std::string_view\f[R], so a
\f[CR]std::string\f[R] goes in directly.
The call reads the bytes in place.
It does not copy the bytes and keeps nothing.
.PP
For a number or a truth value, \f[CR]T\f[R] is an integer type,
\f[CR]double\f[R] or \f[CR]bool\f[R].
A \f[CR]double\f[R] also reads an integer that it holds exactly.
No other value converts: a text read as a number gives
\f[CR]incorrect_type\f[R], and \f[CR]256\f[R] read as
\f[CR]std::uint8_t\f[R] gives \f[CR]number_out_of_range\f[R].
\f[CR]float\f[R] does not compile as \f[CR]T\f[R].
.PP
With \f[CR]std::string\f[R] as \f[CR]T\f[R], the call copies the text of
the value into the result.
That result needs no owner.
.IP
.EX
cbor::at_path<\(dq$.user.name\(dq, std::string>(bytes);   \f[I]// \(dqann\(dq, a copy\f[R]
.EE
.SS 2. Read a text
A text comes back as a view into the bytes.
So the call takes an owner of the bytes, a \f[CR]std::shared_ptr\f[R],
and the result holds it (basics.md, section 2).
.IP
.EX
\f[B]auto\f[R] const owner = std::make_shared<std::string const>(std::move(bytes));
\f[B]auto\f[R] const name = cbor::at_path<\(dq$.user.name\(dq, std::string_view>(owner);
\f[B]if\f[R] (name)
    use(**name);   \f[I]// \(dqann\(dq\f[R]
.EE
.PP
A byte string reads the same way, as
\f[CR]std::span<std::byte const>\f[R].
.SS 3. Start at a lazy
A path can start at a \f[CR]cbor::lazy\f[R] (runtime.md).
\f[CR]$\f[R] is then the value of the lazy.
A text needs no separate owner here, because the lazy holds the bytes.
.IP
.EX
\f[I]// bytes: {\(dqcars\(dq: [{\(dqhp\(dq: 5}, {\(dqhp\(dq: 7, \(dqmake\(dq: \(dqkia\(dq}]}\f[R]
\f[B]auto\f[R] const cars = cbor::lazy::take_ownership(std::move(bytes)).and_then([](cbor::lazy const &doc) { \f[B]return\f[R] doc.at(\(dqcars\(dq); });
\f[B]if\f[R] (cars) {
    cars\->at_path<\(dq$[1].hp\(dq, std::int64_t>();          \f[I]// 7\f[R]
    cars\->at_path<\(dq$[1].make\(dq, std::string_view>();    \f[I]// \(dqkia\(dq\f[R]
}
.EE
.SS 4. Path syntax
The examples select from this value:
.IP
.EX
{\(dqstore\(dq: {\(dqbook\(dq: [{\(dqtitle\(dq: \(dqA\(dq, \(dqprice\(dq: 8},
                    {\(dqtitle\(dq: \(dqB\(dq, \(dqprice\(dq: 12},
                    {\(dqtitle\(dq: \(dqC\(dq, \(dqprice\(dq: 9, \(dqisbn\(dq: \(dqx\(dq}],
           \(dqbicycle\(dq: {\(dqprice\(dq: 20}}}
.EE
.PP
